# Transcript fetching — optional Webshare rotating residential proxy.
#
# WHY: YouTube IP-blocks the transcript (timedtext) endpoint for
# datacenter/cloud/flagged IPs. Metadata (yt-dlp) still works direct, but
# transcript fetches need a clean residential IP. Set these two vars to route
# ONLY the transcript fetch through Webshare; leave them unset to fetch direct
# (fine from an unblocked IP).
#
# SETUP:
#   1. Buy a "Residential" plan at webshare.io (NOT "Proxy Server"/datacenter).
#      1 GB (~$3.50/mo) covers a full backfill many times over — transcripts
#      are tiny text (~300-400 KB/video through the proxy).
#   2. Copy this file to .env and paste your proxy username/password below.
#   3. Nothing to load — every command reads this file itself, from the repo root.
#      (.env is gitignored; never commit real credentials.)

WEBSHARE_PROXY_USERNAME=
WEBSHARE_PROXY_PASSWORD=


# Order execution — required only by `setups --execute`.
#
# WHY HERE AND NOT cfg/: cfg/ is committed. Risk settings live in cfg/execution.yaml where
# they can be read in a diff; the signing key lives here, where .gitignore keeps it out of
# the history. execution/config.py has no field that would accept a key from the YAML, so
# the two cannot be confused.
#
# SETUP:
#   1. Go to app.hyperliquid-testnet.xyz (testnet) and connect a wallet.
#   2. Use the faucet to get mock USDC. Nothing here spends real money while
#      cfg/execution.yaml says `network: testnet`.
#   3. More -> API -> generate an API wallet. USE THAT KEY, NOT YOUR MAIN WALLET'S.
#      An API/agent wallet can place and cancel orders but CANNOT WITHDRAW, which is the
#      entire reason to use one. A leaked agent key costs you open positions; a leaked main
#      key costs you the account.
#   4. Nothing to load. `uv run` does not inherit your shell's exports, so the commands read
#      this file directly (core/env.py) — an exported variable still overrides it.
#
# ACCOUNT_ADDRESS is the account being traded (your wallet address). SECRET_KEY is the API
# wallet's private key — these are two different addresses, and that is expected: the agent
# signs, the account owns.

HYPERLIQUID_ACCOUNT_ADDRESS=
HYPERLIQUID_SECRET_KEY=

# ── Alpaca (US equities, cfg/execution.yaml `venue: alpaca`) ───────────────────
#
# A regulated US broker-dealer, so this is an ordinary brokerage account rather than a
# wallet — funded by ACH from a bank, and crypto cannot fund it. The key pair can place and
# cancel orders but cannot move money out: withdrawals go to a bank on file and are not
# reachable from the trading API, which is the same property that makes a Hyperliquid agent
# wallet the right thing to sign with.
#
#   1. https://app.alpaca.markets/paper/dashboard/overview -> generate a PAPER key.
#   2. Paper and live are different key pairs AND different hosts. A live key will not
#      authenticate against the paper host, which is a feature — the URL is the only thing
#      separating a rehearsal from real money, so it cannot be reached by a wrong flag alone.
#   3. `network: paper` is the default for this venue and `live` needs a typed confirmation.

ALPACA_API_KEY_ID=
ALPACA_API_SECRET_KEY=


# The nightly digest's email — optional. Without these the digest still prints and still
# files its vault note; only the mail is skipped, with a warning naming the missing variable.
#
# WHY SMTP AND NOT THE GMAIL MCP: the MCP server authenticates interactively — it wants a
# browser and a person. The nightly runs under launchd with nobody there, so it is not an
# option however convenient it looks from a Claude Code session.
#
# NOTE THIS IS A SECOND KIND OF SECRET IN THIS FILE. Everything above signs orders; this
# sends mail. A Gmail *app password* is not your Google password — it is scoped to SMTP,
# revocable on its own, and cannot be used to sign in.
#
# SETUP:
#   1. Google Account -> Security -> 2-Step Verification must be on first.
#   2. Then Security -> App passwords -> generate one for "Mail". 16 characters; spaces in
#      it are cosmetic and may be pasted either way.
#   3. DIGEST_TO takes a comma-separated list if you want more than one recipient.
#
# DIGEST_SMTP_HOST and DIGEST_SMTP_PORT default to smtp.gmail.com:587 (STARTTLS) and only
# need setting for a non-Gmail provider.

DIGEST_SMTP_USER=
DIGEST_SMTP_APP_PASSWORD=
DIGEST_TO=


# Brokerage holdings — optional, read-only, and only needed if you want `review` to pull
# positions instead of reading a hand-kept file.
#
# WHY PLAID AND NOT THE BROKER: neither M1 nor Robinhood has a public API for equity
# positions. Robinhood's official API is crypto-only; M1 has none at all. The unofficial
# wrappers work by holding your **password and your 2FA seed**, which is full account
# control including withdrawals, and they breach both brokers' terms. Plaid is OAuth — no
# password reaches this repo, and it is the sanctioned path.
#
# FIRST, CHECK COVERAGE: `uv run python scripts/probe_plaid_coverage.py`. Plaid connects to
# an institution for a *set* of products, and Balance is not Investments — one is a total,
# the other is your positions. An institution Plaid reaches for balance only is useless to
# `review`. Measured 2026-08-28: M1 Finance and Robinhood both answer YES to investments,
# which **contradicts Plaid's own published coverage page for M1**. Trust the probe.
#
# SETUP:
#   1. dashboard.plaid.com/signup. A team created after 2026-04-15 gets the free Trial plan:
#      10 live connections, Investments included.
#   2. Copy the client_id and the **production** secret into the two lines below.
#   3. Pick a use case at dashboard.plaid.com/link/data-transparency-v5. Without it every
#      login refuses with INVALID_LINK_CUSTOMIZATION, which reads like a code bug and is not.
#   4. `uv run plaid-link <portfolio>` per account, then `uv run plaid-sync`.

PLAID_CLIENT_ID=
PLAID_SECRET=

# One per linked account, written by `plaid-link` — you do not type these. The name matches
# the portfolio file, so PLAID_ACCESS_TOKEN_RETIREMENT fills data/portfolios/retirement.yaml.
# Read-only: `plaid-link` requests the `investments` product and nothing else, so the token
# cannot move money. Delete a line to force a re-link; `plaid-link` never overwrites one.
#
# PLAID_ACCESS_TOKEN_RETIREMENT=


# Self-custodied crypto — optional, and the same job as Plaid for a wallet you hold the keys
# to. `wallet-sync` reads the public addresses written into a portfolio file and rewrites its
# `positions:` block from what the chain says.
#
# NOTHING SECRET GOES IN THE PORTFOLIO FILE. A public address is the string you give someone
# to send you funds — it carries no authority, so this path cannot move a coin even in
# principle. It is still a privacy leak, since an address exposes your whole history to anyone
# holding it, which is why the addresses live in gitignored `data/` and not in `cfg/`.
#
# SETUP:
#   1. dashboard.alchemy.com — sign up, create an app, copy its API key here. The free tier is
#      far more than a nightly sync needs, and holdings reads are not metered per position.
#   2. Add a `wallets:` block to a file in data/portfolios/, then `uv run wallet-sync`.
#
# CHECK COVERAGE FIRST: `uv run python scripts/probe_alchemy_wallet.py`. It tries each chain by
# name, which matters more than it sounds: **Alchemy's docs name Solana `sol-mainnet` and the
# API only accepts `solana-mainnet`.** The rejection reads as "not enabled on your key", so it
# sends you to the dashboard to turn on something already on. Probe the name first.

# ALCHEMY_API_KEY=
